#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162128 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea596df6-f52e-5347-afdd-78521b40525e" :knowledge-point/title "Conditional Expectation as a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cfb93cf-bd42-5bbd-b926-c729076a7176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528595} {:answer/id #uuid "9d05d328-ed87-5676-8000-bf330c49b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528676} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e11f88-ae8b-5b32-bcc7-944cd00e9429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528853} {:answer/id #uuid "f86d5c54-dcc9-590b-831d-717992f4b407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528917} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4e3252-f147-5beb-9e9a-fc41e8f66725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cfb93cf-bd42-5bbd-b926-c729076a7176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528595} :answer-field/id #uuid "10b50755-e223-5dd6-8034-940c39f83d0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d9910fd-054f-5969-9b22-8e682fab832a" :question/math-academy-id "q-145622" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & {x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,\\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{x}^{2}), & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{\\frac{3}{2}(1-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,\\,0<x<1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{{x}^{2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{1-{x}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{(1-{x}^{2})}(1+{x}^{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162127 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56c44f33-e6d3-5e34-9d9d-ded24576b82e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Conditional Expectations Given Joint and Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c49c4a-231f-5e98-b371-0e935a20a3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528305} {:answer/id #uuid "92fa2249-5af2-5ed4-b4f5-e80a0891b31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528650} {:answer/id #uuid "a59e2126-217b-588b-8ec1-c18e748c0160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528701} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e58acf-da9e-5137-9d01-db3f88e60768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528788} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d6410d-8ce9-57d1-9928-c24c78a23343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7d6410d-8ce9-57d1-9928-c24c78a23343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528837} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a449ce-c8fa-55c9-ab8d-6d9f7f6b2000" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0901d8a-5ab9-5481-aeea-fb9811603032" :question/math-academy-id "q-146256" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+y)}{4}, & 0<x\\le 2y,\\,\\,0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3(x+y)}{4})}{3{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+y)}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<x\\le 2y,\\,0<y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$ \n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{(x+1)}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{(x+1)}{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{8}{3}+2)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162127 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56c44f33-e6d3-5e34-9d9d-ded24576b82e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Conditional Expectations Given Joint and Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ef324f-e3e0-55de-bf8c-31d0adf63779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528317} {:answer/id #uuid "1433506f-2bda-51e6-9d54-5354fc116b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528350} {:answer/id #uuid "1d68d2b6-b709-52d4-a640-81aa11b499e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528380} {:answer/id #uuid "76810779-d00b-5526-95a9-f6b537df2534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528574} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4f0db2-b37d-5e05-b26f-af89dbb0df5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76810779-d00b-5526-95a9-f6b537df2534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528574} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e89487-9f71-5b83-9a7d-94bccae434fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "febf3d13-3a80-5fb8-bb8d-fc4ac7755bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-146344" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1+y-x, & 0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,\\,0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0<y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+y-x)}{2y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$ \n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{(2-x)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{(2-x)}{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(4-\\frac{8}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162127 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56c44f33-e6d3-5e34-9d9d-ded24576b82e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Conditional Expectations Given Joint and Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052804b5-ac12-5294-a262-e3fa2b590da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528315} {:answer/id #uuid "31321875-45ba-5d60-9460-76f9ff4b65bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528422} {:answer/id #uuid "66deb24d-93e5-58be-9017-317a664a99a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528546} {:answer/id #uuid "c902eb5b-0235-5da3-b574-a9735354dbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528792} {:answer/id #uuid "d7217da2-ae78-508a-874f-f889c1b07a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7217da2-ae78-508a-874f-f889c1b07a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528836} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b89aa0-9d44-5e08-ab59-3cc05bb04a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72ca9ca6-7469-59a8-9b29-546c35450d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-146353" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2+y-x}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-2x}{6}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2+y-x}{3})}{(\\frac{5-2x}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2+y-x)}{5-2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{2(2+y-1)}{5-2\\cdot 1}=\\frac{2(1+y)}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2(1+y)}{3}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
